And I don't argue with that because it makes sense.

392
00:35:20,150 --> 00:35:34,870
But sometimes that makes it harder to use it from C because this documentation is often yeah the types and the structures and the R side when they tell Rush to represent that as C you just have to kind of see what comes out don't you?

393
00:35:34,880 --> 00:35:37,270
Yeah.

394
00:35:37,270 --> 00:35:45,270
And then it comes back to this to do this really well you probably need to understand quite a lot of Rust and C at the same time and that's a special intersection of people.

395
00:35:45,280 --> 00:35:47,589
Excellent.

396
00:35:47,589 --> 00:35:52,631
So I'm going to move us on to our what you call autopsy for one of a better word. What went wrong?

397
00:35:52,631 --> 00:35:55,670
You know the analysis here.

398
00:35:55,670 --> 00:36:00,230
So first question, was this the right part of curl to use rust in?

399
00:36:00,230 --> 00:36:02,470
Yes, I think it could have been the right part to use rust in.

400
00:36:02,470 --> 00:36:07,990
I don't think it was actually the part of curl that was the problem because I think as I said before,

401
00:36:07,990 --> 00:36:16,550
I think we can pretty much introduce backends for more or less anything in curl like this if we just spend time and energy and do it correctly.

402
00:36:16,550 --> 00:36:18,870
So I don't think it was the part of curl that was the problem.

403
00:36:18,870 --> 00:36:28,550
I think the primary thing that made us eventually pull it out again is that we didn't really find users that wanted it.

404
00:36:28,560 --> 00:36:42,069
Yeah. So we have this I kind of like the the sort of the survival of the fittest approach by offering different backends for different solutions so that users would pick whatever they want and go with that.

405
00:36:42,069 --> 00:36:52,790
You know, we offer bunch of different TLS library solutions, for example, and users can pick whatever they want and build curl and and use that and we can over time detect that wait a minute, these different ones are never used.

406
00:36:52,790 --> 00:36:54,471
We don't have to support them anymore. We can remove them.

407
00:36:54,471 --> 00:37:02,390
So, pretty much letting users decide what we should have and what's used and what's best and what they want.

408
00:37:02,390 --> 00:37:12,630
It was quite notable quite early on and through the entire project that we had a hard time to get anyone to actually even just try out the hyper backend.

409
00:37:12,630 --> 00:37:25,829
So we had a pretty much no one used it and by not having users we also had very few contributors you know helping out improving the back end so that very few people actually engaged and helped us out.

410
00:37:25,829 --> 00:37:29,510
So how do you figure out if a back end's being used or is popular or not?

411
00:37:29,520 --> 00:37:32,630
basically just by asking people.

412
00:37:32,630 --> 00:37:46,630
So we have annual user surveys and usually you know asking on the mailing list people are frequent users are you using it or sometimes you could just notice you know by when we have had a serious bug for a while and we fix it and no one has told us about it.

413
00:37:46,630 --> 00:37:48,790
That's a sort of a pretty big clue that no one actually used it.

414
00:37:48,800 --> 00:38:00,470
We also marked it as experimental which also of course is a blocker for people to use it in production because we never actually got it completely 100% the same as the native.

415
00:38:00,470 --> 00:38:09,670
So we kept having I think like 3 4% of the test cases disabled because we had when you enabled hyper because we never got those test cases working exactly the same.

416
00:38:09,680 --> 00:38:21,829
Yeah, I suppose you could argue if it was marked as stable and then it became the default option then you'd get bug reports and people would opt in would help out but if you didn't get there you didn't get there.

417
00:38:21,839 --> 00:38:23,991
Exactly. Yeah.

418
00:38:23,991 --> 00:38:28,950
Of course that's sort of that was one obstacle certainly a major one.

419
00:38:28,950 --> 00:38:32,470
And were the other Rust options, the TLS stuff, was that there before you started this project?

420
00:38:32,470 --> 00:38:34,390
So you had some experience or was that done after?

421
00:38:34,400 --> 00:38:44,630
The key one for HP3 that was started before the Russell's one started I think pretty much while I was doing this.

422
00:38:44,640 --> 00:38:46,150
So that's Rust TLS, isn't it?

423
00:38:46,160 --> 00:38:48,870
Rust TLS.

424
00:38:48,870 --> 00:38:58,550
We have a much better understanding of TLS in curl and it makes it's we have much better infrastructure and setup for TLS backends in curl.

425
00:38:58,550 --> 00:39:12,390
So I think that was so that's a smoother ride and a better fit into curl and also I think TLS is a better abstraction layer I think maybe than HTTP sort of quote unquote because what the internal API

426
00:39:12,390 --> 00:39:15,829
or how to abstract HTTP away it's certainly far from clear

427
00:39:15,839 --> 00:39:27,750
and uh obvious how to do it and one of the challenges we face many times I already sort of alluded a little bit to the that hyper and curl has a little bit of different

428
00:39:27,750 --> 00:39:34,790
we have different sort of approaches how we approach HP which made it for example when getting the headers from hyper it would get them in a more

429
00:39:34,800 --> 00:39:47,829
or less random order while curl always returns them in the order they were sent over the wire which of course if I don't have the same order I can't compare them correctly as I do in my current test suite so that's one thing for example

430
00:39:47,839 --> 00:39:56,150
and other things like hyper has certainly other limits restrictions requirements on the HTTP layer than curl

431
00:39:56,160 --> 00:40:08,470
which also introduced funny friction and differences when you built one way or the other way because they would behave differently depending on just policies or religion or taste.

432
00:40:08,480 --> 00:40:16,550
The existing Rust things that you're using were again just C libraries but in a wellestablished landscape because you had other backends already.

433
00:40:16,560 --> 00:40:19,110
Yes. And this just this compacted it.

434
00:40:19,119 --> 00:40:26,790
And there must have been a point or was there a point when you thought, hold on, we're having to make so many changes to Hyper to get this to work.

435
00:40:26,790 --> 00:40:32,069
Did you ever have a conversation about, well, let's just do this from scratch because you tried it out or was that just not a thing?

436
00:40:32,079 --> 00:40:35,910
I think that also because I'm not qualified.

437
00:40:35,910 --> 00:40:38,230
I'm not skilled enough in Rust myself to do that.

438
00:40:38,230 --> 00:40:42,950
So, I wouldn't do it and therefore I wouldn't dream of demanding it from someone else either.

439
00:40:42,960 --> 00:40:51,510
So it was pretty much hyper or not at all right now at least sort of because now we had this hyper solution.

440
00:40:51,510 --> 00:40:56,470
This is what we struggle to get working and I think we got it pretty far as well.

441
00:40:56,470 --> 00:41:01,670
So I mean we were at 90 I don't know 97% of test case coverage.

442
00:41:01,670 --> 00:41:10,790
So I think we were pretty well on our way to get it to work but then it flatlined and no one worked on it for a while.

443
00:41:10,790 --> 00:41:15,589
just sat at that state and we didn't seem to have anyone really interested in pushing it forward.

444
00:41:15,599 --> 00:41:25,829
And when it was pretty much at that state, we decided that wait a minute, if if this is where we are, no one is pushing for it and no one is really asking for it either.

445
00:41:25,829 --> 00:41:31,270
And all of these different caveats and we need to do these different maybe not clutchy,

446
00:41:31,270 --> 00:41:39,030
but maybe some sometimes a little bit of uncomfortable or quirky solutions internally.

447
00:41:39,030 --> 00:41:42,630
And still we're only at this point.

448
00:41:42,630 --> 00:41:50,069
So maybe that's what we should call a sort of a end of the road for this adventure and say that we didn't actually manage to do this in a proper way.

449
00:41:50,079 --> 00:41:58,150
Um that brings me nicely on to what benefits did you gain from the attempt which to me sound like a lot you know a lot of things that you've learned.

450
00:41:58,160 --> 00:42:00,630
I think we learned a lot.

451
00:42:00,630 --> 00:42:02,630
I think actually Hyper also learned a lot.

452
00:42:02,630 --> 00:42:05,030
I think they improved as well as we did.

453
00:42:05,040 --> 00:42:15,510
So even though when we sort of split off and we're no longer using hyper I think we improved curl in several ways we made the HTTP handling more rigid

454
00:42:15,510 --> 00:42:28,790
and and stringent to match up with hyper really and we did a lot of cleaning up internally to be able to do this backend separation and that helped us internally to just do HTTP in a cleaner way and

455
00:42:28,800 --> 00:42:42,710
then also this lesson that we learned that we how we should do this the next time pretty And this entire hyper journey also coincided with other refactors we've done in curl.

456
00:42:42,710 --> 00:42:52,470
And I think the hyper lesson also helped us make decisions and and learn from that and use that when we refactored the internals in curl.

457
00:42:52,470 --> 00:43:03,030
So that we sort of have in the back of our minds we now have this idea that when or maybe if we do this again now we have these different tools and and setups that will help us do this better the next time.

458
00:43:03,040 --> 00:43:10,470
Did any of the changes that you made to to bring in Hyper, did they remain or did you just rip them all out when you removed Hyper?

459
00:43:10,480 --> 00:43:22,150
A lot of the improvements we did to HTTP in general and and the separation of HTTP from other there were some cases of pretty much of um I don't know how to sort of explain it

460
00:43:22,150 --> 00:43:32,950
but you know that we have sort of infected other code parts with HTTP specific knowledge that we had to make sure that they were not in the generic parts they had to be in the HTTP parts.

461
00:43:32,950 --> 00:43:38,630
So we did a lot of cleanups like that that have certainly remained and just been emphasized and improved over time.

462
00:43:38,630 --> 00:43:44,309
So I would say that quite a lot of the HTTP related the protocol related parts they have remained.

463
00:43:44,309 --> 00:43:53,270
What we pretty much ripped out was the entire attempt to have this flexible backend approach where we go either or way because we don't have that anymore.

464
00:43:53,270 --> 00:43:56,230
So we don't have to provide it. So that part we ripped out.

465
00:43:56,240 --> 00:43:58,309
That makes sense.

466
00:43:58,309 --> 00:44:03,191
In your blog post, you said, so I'm just going to quote this bit. 95% of the work is the easy part.

467
00:44:03,200 --> 00:44:14,069
Can you explain the other sentence that says the final few% would however turn out to be frictional enough to now eventually make us admit defeat, give up, and instead yank it all out again.

468
00:44:14,079 --> 00:44:21,430
So what those last bits I think pretty much because we made everything.

469
00:44:21,430 --> 00:44:24,230
We have about 2,000 test cases in total.

470
00:44:24,230 --> 00:44:26,309
I think about 800 of them or something.

471
00:44:26,309 --> 00:44:39,190
a thousand are about HTTP and I think we had we were down to maybe 15 of them failing with Hyper and getting those final 15 test cases to work.

472
00:44:39,190 --> 00:44:54,150
Those were the ones that sort of finally broke us because they require that someone actually understand hyper probably the C glue layer and curl and the protocols involved and sometimes more protocols involved

473
00:44:54,150 --> 00:45:04,710
because sometimes they also involve actually in these cases many times they involve HTTP authentication mechanisms too like NLM or digest or negotiate horrible things.

474
00:45:04,720 --> 00:45:15,030
So you really need particular kind of person with intimate knowledge of all this and not only knowledge but you also have one

475
00:45:15,030 --> 00:45:26,630
you need an interest and an energy enough to actually dig into this and work on this and we basically didn't find those persons with that time and energy.

476
00:45:26,630 --> 00:45:29,109
So they remained there and I didn't see any easy outcome.

477
00:45:29,109 --> 00:45:33,349
I didn't see anyone likely to show up to do this work.

478
00:45:33,349 --> 00:45:41,030
So those 15 tests, do you recall a couple of them what they were and and why they failed and well why they failed?

479
00:45:41,030 --> 00:45:44,790
That's part of the reason why if we knew exactly why they failed, it would have been easier.

480
00:45:44,790 --> 00:45:47,990
But that was part of the research that would have been done.

481
00:45:47,990 --> 00:45:55,750
It's in the in the interaction or the differences between the hyper solution to do things to the native solution to do things.

482
00:45:55,750 --> 00:46:01,750
And I could also just add that hyper is not doing everything.

483
00:46:01,750 --> 00:46:04,630
curl can do a lot more HTTP than hyper can.

484
00:46:04,630 --> 00:46:09,829
So the HTTP back end Hyper provides was not everything HTTP.

485
00:46:09,829 --> 00:46:12,470
So there was still part of HTTP that curl did natively.

486
00:46:12,470 --> 00:46:14,470
For example, all those authentication mechanisms,

487
00:46:14,470 --> 00:46:23,990
there was this interaction or middle where curl would do set up things magically to how

488
00:46:23,990 --> 00:46:32,550
to do HTTP and when it would use and interact with Hyper to perform the HTTP requests, but also it's not easy to explain and I don't recall the details right now,

489
00:46:32,550 --> 00:46:47,030
but there were mostly cases like that and they would end up slightly different than you know the requests or the responses would be slightly different depending on which solution you would use And the entire challenge there would be to figure out why and and what to do to fix it.

490
00:46:47,040 --> 00:46:55,670
I think I can imagine because I've done similar in my C project bringing Rust into it which is another reason why I was interested.

491
00:46:55,670 --> 00:47:00,951
So I imagine that the bits that curl still does that Hyper doesn't or never will. You set those up.

492
00:47:00,960 --> 00:47:03,109
You're giving your data.

493
00:47:03,109 --> 00:47:07,750
So you're passing stuff that's being created is in memory.

494
00:47:07,750 --> 00:47:12,870
you know, is all aligned or what have you on on the curl C side of the FFI boundary.

495
00:47:12,870 --> 00:47:15,109
You shove that across to Rust.

496
00:47:15,109 --> 00:47:25,349
It has to then interpret and understand all that bit correctly, do its bit, make changes, and then pass stuff back across the FFI boundary from Rust to C and hope that bit doesn't get messed up.

497
00:47:25,349 --> 00:47:32,950
And then you've got the strict agreements of who's managing memory, who's doing what, who's freeing things, who's setting it up, and that can get complicated very quickly.

498
00:47:32,960 --> 00:47:35,589
Yes.

499
00:47:35,589 --> 00:47:40,230
So that type of interaction because it was hyper is not a full 100% replacement for what Carl is doing.

500
00:47:40,240 --> 00:47:42,950
Exactly.

501
00:47:42,950 --> 00:47:45,430
And by the fact that it is doing things differently.

502
00:47:45,430 --> 00:47:47,910
So it was also that nature.

503
00:47:47,910 --> 00:47:51,430
So it was just you know fairly complicated programming challenge.

504
00:47:51,430 --> 00:47:57,589
I mean not impossible of course but it was just challenging enough that it turned out to be an obstacle that no one really worked on.

505
00:47:57,599 --> 00:48:07,589
And when no one has had worked on it for long enough, I took it as a sign that it's probably not something people want to work on either.

506
00:48:07,589 --> 00:48:09,510
And no one showed up interested in doing it either.

507
00:48:09,510 --> 00:48:19,990
Well, people did work on it that helped us out and and we got this far thanks not only to me of course or to Sean, but actually also to a whole bunch of other contributors who helped out.

508
00:48:19,990 --> 00:48:22,069
So there were people around.

509
00:48:22,069 --> 00:48:25,510
I mean, you hold very very high standards obviously because it's your baby.

510
00:48:25,510 --> 00:48:27,430
But you could argue that it's a success.

511
00:48:27,430 --> 00:48:33,191
You know, you got that far and you had 15 tests left to solve. I mean, that's a pretty big win, wasn't it? Really?

512
00:48:33,200 --> 00:48:37,030
I mean, I think we proved that it actually could work.

513
00:48:37,030 --> 00:48:43,030
It proved that the idea was solid and the implementation wasn't perfect, but it worked.

514
00:48:43,030 --> 00:48:49,990
So, I think in that aspect, it was a win, but then in the end, we didn't go all the way.

515
00:48:49,990 --> 00:48:58,549
And I was also the fact that we didn't really have anyone asking for this or using this or pushing for this, you know, there was no demand from the user side either.

516
00:48:58,549 --> 00:49:04,390
That was also sort of a pretty big factor because there was no, you know, big gain in the end of all this struggle either.

517
00:49:04,400 --> 00:49:06,710
Yeah.

518
00:49:06,710 --> 00:49:09,670
It's a feature build that if no one's asking for it, you know, it's a feature really, isn't it?

519
00:49:09,680 --> 00:49:11,750
Right.

520
00:49:11,750 --> 00:49:23,829
And so in the end it felt like yeah there's final friction here that we could maybe surpass and go through if we really made an effort but there was no one asking us to do that and the big trophy in the end was missing.

521
00:49:23,839 --> 00:49:28,791
Did you have a timeline by the sponsors of this feature? I forget the acronym.

522
00:49:28,800 --> 00:49:33,030
Did they say you had a year five years or just how did that work?

