00:49:35,430 --> 00:49:37,990
They were much more flexible in ISRG.

525
00:49:37,990 --> 00:49:40,230
So they were really great with this.

526
00:49:40,230 --> 00:49:49,910
So they were fully on board with this and I had a full communication with them during over the years really and even when we came to this conclusion it wasn't really a conclusion first.

527
00:49:49,910 --> 00:49:57,190
It was more like a thought that maybe we should just conclude defeat here and give up and rip it out again.

528
00:49:57,190 --> 00:50:03,270
So I had conversation with them all the time and they were very good with this and they understand it completely.

529
00:50:03,270 --> 00:50:10,150
they struggle quite a lot too to find people to actually you know pick up the ball and continue this work and in the end I think we all concluded

530
00:50:10,160 --> 00:50:20,549
that this is the right decision for now I mean we haven't shut the door for this idea or this effort or this concept for the future

531
00:50:20,549 --> 00:50:32,390
anyway so who knows maybe we will come back to this idea and restart it in the future yeah there might be some trigger that comes out and it's just a a no-brainer to pick it up that's the beauty of source control you've got all the history there.

532
00:50:32,390 --> 00:50:33,910
I think I've asked this already or we've touched it.

533
00:50:33,910 --> 00:50:35,589
What can others learn from this?

534
00:50:35,599 --> 00:50:45,510
The general lesson is basically that it's it is a fair approach to replace C++ code in a larger project.

535
00:50:45,510 --> 00:50:49,910
So you can I kind of always like that approach with Rust already back from the days I worked

536
00:50:49,920 --> 00:51:02,870
with Firefox that it's it's a convenient and decent way to replace components sort of component by component wise instead of having to rewrite everything into Rust in one single blow.

537
00:51:02,870 --> 00:51:04,950
You can instead do it component by component.

538
00:51:04,950 --> 00:51:11,190
I and I still think that's a good way to do it and I think we proved that it works to do it that way.

539
00:51:11,190 --> 00:51:24,470
other things I I think it's harder because then you get into the weeds of exactly how the API works and how to actually replace a component in a software and that depends on the particular software I think more than anything else.

540
00:51:24,470 --> 00:51:34,790
I think we've covered my last question which would be why didn't others step forward to help and you explained that the knowledge domain here is like four different things

541
00:51:34,790 --> 00:51:43,510
you know there's the rust way to do it there's the C interface there's the particular bit you're replacing on curl

542
00:51:43,510 --> 00:51:51,030
and then the protocols or the specs involved with that whether it's authentication or you know HTTP version 4 when that comes out etc. Yeah,

543
00:51:51,030 --> 00:51:57,670
and I think just the general lack of a perceived upside of of using this.

544
00:51:57,670 --> 00:52:03,990
I don't think most people think curl's native solution is bad or insecure and anything.

545
00:52:03,990 --> 00:52:08,950
So people wouldn't sort of dream of switching over to this new one anyway.

546
00:52:08,950 --> 00:52:13,030
So we're stuck in the land where people using curl, they they're pretty satisfied with the existing solution.

547
00:52:13,040 --> 00:52:17,031
People using Hyper, they're mostly Rust users. They're they're in Rust land.

548
00:52:17,040 --> 00:52:18,710
They're using Hyper already.

549
00:52:18,710 --> 00:52:21,430
They don't use curl anyway.

550
00:52:21,430 --> 00:52:24,710
So we ended up in this middle land with no demand.

551
00:52:24,720 --> 00:52:27,109
Yeah.

552
00:52:27,109 --> 00:52:31,109
I suppose if this was the hot spot of memory related security issues or what have you,

553
00:52:31,109 --> 00:52:39,750
you'd either take the decision to address them and see and fix them like you're constantly doing refactoring to make improvements or you bring rust in to do it.

554
00:52:39,750 --> 00:52:46,470
But without the sort of raw data to say you know given x amount of effort you'll get y amount of return it's hard to really quantify it.

555
00:52:46,480 --> 00:52:56,950
Yeah, we can also come back to the fact that my plan is not to remove the native C alternative anyway because I think the portability of curl

